<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Exploring Our Global Community – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> A Dream Trip Around the World</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe experiences from a cultural exchange. Interactive: Conduct interviews on general interest topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe cultural exchange experiences, noting specific learning points (e.g., "On my cultural exchange, I adapted to new customs, like greeting with a kiss on the cheek and respecting local food traditions.")
Can conduct interviews to explore cultural interests and perspectives about traveling and learning from other cultures (e.g., "If you could travel anywhere in the future, where would you go?" "If I traveled to Paris, I would explore the history and architecture." "If you visited another country, which one would you choose?" "If I visited Japan, I would experience the cherry blossom festivals, and if I went to the USA, I would explore historical landmarks like the Smithsonian museums in Washington D.C.")
Can respond to interview questions with thoughtful reflections (e.g., "How was your trip abroad influencing your understanding of different cultures and global challenges?" "During the trip, I was noticing cultural differences and learning about global issues, especially how climate change was affecting communities worldwide.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 60%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the speaking focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of breathtaking global destinations (e.g., pyramids, beaches, mountains, waterfalls). Students are asked: "If you could visit any of these places, or anywhere else in the world, which one would you choose and why?" Students share briefly with a partner, then a few share with the class. This activates prior knowledge and introduces hypothetical travel.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing a dream trip using the second conditional, superlatives, and demonstrating the pronunciation of stressed/unstressed syllables in modals and multisyllabic words. For example: "If I could travel around the world, I would visit Egypt. I would explore the magnificent pyramids, which I think are the most stunning wonders of the ancient world. If I visited Japan, I would experience the cherry blossom festivals. I believe that would be the most unforgettable experience." (Teacher exaggerates stress on words like TRAVEL, WORLD, VISIT, PYRAMIDS, adVENture, des-ti-NA-tion).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'If I could travel, I would visit Egypt,' am I talking about a real trip I'm taking tomorrow or a hypothetical, dream trip?" (Expected answer: A dream/hypothetical trip.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'most stunning' mean when comparing places?" (Expected answer: It means it's the most beautiful or impressive among all others.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of an interview (dialogue format) between two friends discussing hypothetical cultural exchange experiences and dream destinations. The dialogue prominently features second conditional, superlatives, past continuous, and target vocabulary (e.g., <i>Exchange Program, traditions, cultural, diverse, enriching, benefits, understanding, perspectives</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner comprehends and extracts general and specific information from oral texts to understand cultural interests and perspectives about traveling and learning from other cultures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the interview once for gist: "What general topic are the two friends discussing?" (Expected answer: Dream trips and cultural exchange experiences.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen a second time and answer specific True/False questions based on the dialogue. Example T/F statements: "Speaker A would visit Japan for its history." (False - she would visit for cherry blossoms). "Speaker B believes cultural exchange programs offer personal benefits." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the second listening, students orally identify key vocabulary words heard in the dialogue related to travel and culture.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking, practicing specific language points.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the second conditional for hypothetical travel, superlatives for comparing places, and past continuous to describe experiences (real or hypothetical) during a trip. Focus on vocabulary: <i>travel, culture, experience, diversity, adventure, destination, trip, pyramids, beaches, mountains, world, waterfall, wonders, understanding, perspectives, skills, benefits, Exchange Program, traditions; explore, compare, travel, visit, experience, learn, enjoy, hike, encounter, enhance, develop, meet, understand, broaden, narrate, dream, reflect, gain, discover, share; breathtaking, global, romantic, natural, diverse, cultural, beautiful, unforgettable, personal, year-long, enriching, different, unique, educational, stunning, exciting, magnificent, remote, distant, traditional</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Choral Repetition):</b> The teacher provides sentences with gaps focusing on the target grammar. Students complete them individually and then practice saying them aloud in small groups.
                        <br><i>Examples:</i> "If I ________ (can) visit any country, I ________ (would/visit) Japan." "The Amazon is ________ (breathtaking) natural wonder in South America." "While I ________ (travel/past continuous) through the ancient city, I ________ (observe/past continuous) local traditions."
                        <br>Teacher listens for correct grammar and pronunciation of vowel reduction in "could" and "would," and stress in multisyllabic words like "adVENture."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Substitution Drill):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence frames and lists of words for substitution (e.g., countries, activities, adjectives).
                        <br><i>Frame:</i> "If I visited [Country], I would [Verb] [Adjective] [Noun]."
                        <br><i>List:</i> (Country: Japan, Egypt, USA) (Verb: explore, experience, hike) (Adjective: unique, historical, breathtaking) (Noun: culture, pyramids, mountains).
                        <br>Students take turns creating sentences orally. Example: "If I visited Japan, I would experience its unique culture." "If I visited Egypt, I would explore the historical pyramids."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Question & Answer Practice):</b> Students generate two second conditional questions about dream travel and cultural experiences. They then interview their partner, focusing on polite discussion of cultural differences.
                        <br><i>Example questions:</i> "If you could participate in a year-long Exchange Program, where would you go?" "What is one tradition you found interesting while you were traveling (hypothetically)?" "What would be the most enriching part of that adventure?" Partners give feedback on clarity and use of target vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play / Interview Simulation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students will conduct "Cultural Exchange Interviews" in pairs, simulating a discussion about hypothetical travel experiences and their impact on understanding global communities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form pairs. One student acts as an interviewer (e.g., from a travel magazine or cultural exchange organization) and the other as a traveler who has just returned from a *hypothetical* cultural exchange or dream trip.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The interviewer asks at least 4-5 questions about the traveler's "experience," focusing on cultural learning, destinations, and personal growth. They must use the second conditional for hypothetical situations. (e.g., "If you could revisit any destination from your trip, where would it be?" "How was your trip abroad influencing your understanding of different cultures and global challenges?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The traveler responds, describing their experiences using second conditional (for reflections/future hypotheticals), superlatives (for comparing places/experiences), and past continuous (to narrate experiences during their hypothetical trip). They should incorporate at least 5-7 target vocabulary words and show thoughtful reflections. (e.g., "If I had visited Japan, I would experience the cherry blossom festivals, and if I went to the USA, I would explore historical landmarks like the Smithsonian museums in Washington D.C." "During the trip, I was noticing cultural differences and learning about global issues, especially how climate change was affecting communities worldwide." "The benefits of this year-long Exchange Program were immense; it broadened my perspectives.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students swap roles after 3-4 minutes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, observing fluency, collaborative interaction, and use of target language. Teacher also ensures students are respectfully discussing cultural differences and showing interest in classmates' stories (Pragmatic function).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the "Cultural Exchange Interview" to note students' use of target grammar, vocabulary, pronunciation, and interaction skills.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partners provide brief verbal feedback to each other based on a simplified rubric focusing on clarity and active listening.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially vowel reduction in modals and stress on multisyllabic words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of second conditional, superlatives, and past continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words related to travel and culture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in describing experiences and answering questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction and showing interest in partner's stories.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about describing cultural experiences today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel confident/need more practice with (circle one): using the second conditional / using superlatives / narrating with past continuous."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection, linking to future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's interview activity did you enjoy the most, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has discussing dream trips and cultural exchanges broadened your perspective on global diversity?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., Students effectively used the second conditional to express hypothetical travel. The interview format encouraged genuine interaction and use of new vocabulary.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with differentiating past continuous for real vs. hypothetical narratives; review this briefly. Encourage more complex sentence structures for B1.3 level. Ensure more focus on specific cultural traditions in responses.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today to write a descriptive paragraph or an email to a friend, narrating their dream cultural exchange experience or reflections on global travel.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>